+
+## B109 — Reduce cold mutation-campaign cost with explicit, witnessed early-stop execution
+
+**Status: OPEN (filed 2026-09-27 after review of B105's measured runtime; outcome policy decided in A-464).**
+
+**Observed limit:** B105 has 3,760 candidates and runs the full 5,831-test
+suite for every candidate. Its first 11 completed candidates averaged 547
+seconds each, projecting to roughly 23.7 days at one worker. Candidate-ID
+sharding can add concurrency but each candidate still runs the full suite;
+partitioning candidate source files has the same total test work and uneven
+shards. Those are scheduling changes, not a reduction in work. Per-test
+coverage selection could reduce work for survivors, but line reachability by
+itself does not prove that omitted tests cannot observe shared state changes,
+fixtures, imports, or subprocess effects.
+
+### Desired behavior
+
+- Add an explicit opt-in cold-campaign execution mode that can stop a mutant's
+  test run after a trustworthy, attributed test-call failure proves an
+  existential kill. Keep today's full-suite execution as the default and the
+  fallback for every uncertain case.
+- Preserve the full passing baseline, complete candidate discovery, candidate
+  identity, pristine per-candidate snapshots, and the original collected test
+  order. Do not use elapsed time, host load, or scheduling to decide which
+  candidates/tests run or what bucket an outcome receives.
+- Accept an early kill only from a bounded witness that names a collected test
+  node, proves the failure occurred in the test call phase, and records
+  matching pytest-session and child-process exit status 1. Collection/import
+  failures, setup/teardown failures, hooks with ambiguous attribution,
+  subprocess outcomes that cannot be tied to the call, and malformed or
+  missing receipts require a fresh full-suite run.
+- Run the full suite for survivors and whenever no trustworthy early witness
+  is found. Account for every candidate exactly once in the completed native
+  verdict; preserve the selected execution mode and bounded evidence in the
+  resume state and verifier-accepted verdict.
+- Implement A-464's explicit opt-in policy: a certified test-call failure is
+  sufficient to report `killed`, even though omitted later tests might
+  otherwise hang or crash. Keep full-suite execution as the default and run
+  the full suite for survivors and every uncertain witness. Give cold
+  early-stop evidence a distinct execution mode from B106's prior-verdict
+  witness replay; specify its lane, verdict, and resume-state contract rather
+  than overloading B106's `witness-prefix` record.
+- Treat coverage-based test selection for survivors as a separate follow-up
+  design unless an independent proof establishes that its dependency closure
+  covers collection, imports, fixtures, test order, shared state, dynamic
+  dispatch, and subprocess effects. Unknown or incomplete impact evidence
+  always falls back to the full suite.
+
+### Acceptance
+
+- [ ] A deterministic pilot on a fixed source tree compares full execution
+      against the proposed mode over a predeclared sample of candidates and
+      failure positions. It reports per-bucket time saved, full-suite fallback
+      rate, and candidates for which the mode gives no speedup; it does not
+      substitute elapsed-time estimates for completed evidence.
+- [ ] Paired fixtures prove that a genuine test-call failure with both exit
+      statuses equal to 1 can certify a kill, while collection, import,
+      setup, teardown, hook, subprocess, malformed-receipt, and no-witness
+      cases fall back to a full run or remain incomplete.
+- [ ] An adversarial fixture where an early test fails but a later test hangs
+      or crashes demonstrates the selected outcome policy and cannot silently
+      produce a different bucket from the declared contract.
+- [ ] Resume and shard tests prove mode identity is bound to state, every
+      candidate remains represented exactly once, and no prefix receipt can
+      be mistaken for a B106 prior-verdict replay receipt.
+- [ ] `assay verify` independently validates the closed execution mode,
+      policy, witness, candidate inventory, and producer/session receipts; the
+      packaged schema and verifier agree on their locally expressible rules.
+- [ ] README plainly says that an early call-phase failure is enough to mark
+      an opted-in candidate `killed`, and that omitted later tests could have
+      hung or crashed under a full run. DESIGN-GUIDE records A-464, why the
+      existential kill is sound, and the rejected partitioning or coverage
+      shortcuts. CONSUMERS.md has a pasteable opt-in example and describes
+      full-suite execution for survivors and uncertain witnesses. Any schema
+      or public vocabulary change is reflected in all three documents before
+      merge.
