 | A-461 | **B106 is a verdict-schema v13 cut only; lane schema and `ReasonCode` stay unchanged.** Reuse the existing `mutation.candidate_ids` wire field as the candidate-scope inventory: a completed unsharded native run records the full discovered plan, a shard records its selected slice, and `[]` means an empty submitted scope (zero candidates or an empty shard). The pre-submission `MUTANT_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` sentinel omits this inventory, retains its existing `candidate_count = max_mutants + 1` / `total = 0` shape, and is never a reuse source. Every native outcome records `candidate_id`, `source_sha256`, `mutated_file_sha256`, and closed execution provenance (`full` or `witness-prefix`); ingested outcomes carry none of these B106 fields. A pure candidate-ID helper is shared by planning, execution and verify, while verify independently checks producer-dependent presence, field shape, uniqueness/disjointness, inventory equality, and execution-mode relationships. Witness receipts contain only bounded node IDs, call-phase failure, and pytest session/process exit statuses; both exits must be 1. A witness-prefix outcome also binds the exact prior verdict bytes by SHA-256 and records prior/current node IDs.** | GPT-6-Luna xhigh recommended verdict fields only and its follow-up design review confirmed candidate identity inputs, shard inventory scope, and agreement between receipt and ordinary pytest exit. The review found a v12 compatibility conflict: the current verifier hard-rejects every foreign schema version before reconstruction, so it cannot validate a v12 artifact after the v13 hard cut. Choose a bounded duplicate-key-rejecting parse of a v12 JSON object as a cold start, trust only `schema_version = 12`, consume no candidate fields, and run every current candidate fully. This keeps `assay verify`'s hard cut intact; shipping a second frozen v12 verifier would enlarge the legacy trust surface without making v12 candidate witnesses reusable. For v13, current-verifier acceptance precedes source inspection. The current baseline must pass before current inventory discovery and candidate replay; only the unique current witness node's call-phase failure plus session exit 1 and child process exit 1 can certify a replayed kill. Any uncertainty receives a fresh full execution from a new process and pristine candidate snapshot. |
 | A-462 | **B105 adds a separately invoked, full-source R0-R3 `self-qualification` gate inside `tester-unified`; the ordinary `tester-unified` release lane stays R0-only.** The qualification lane names every production `src/assay/**/*.py` target, uses `whole_target` with required branch measurement and a 100% floor, runs every native Python operator serially and unsharded, executes an import-break canary, resumes from gitignored state and emits progress, and succeeds only when the complete verifier-accepted report is bound to the unchanged source commit/tree. A registered R0/R1 preflight uses the same source inventory and stops the full gate before R2 if baseline tests or coverage fail. | Putting full-source mutation on every release would add a large, unrelated cost to the ordinary release contract and repeat a campaign when no full qualification is required. A separately invoked lane makes the stronger claim available without changing release cadence. Its campaign has no total-budget success path: a sentinel, partial inventory, interrupted run, survivor, or invalid report cannot pass. Per-candidate liveness is a failure bound, not a source of positive evidence; scheduling may affect elapsed time, but never changes which complete candidate inventory must be judged. A-462 supersedes A-133's permanent R0-only claim only for this second lane, preserving A-133's ordinary release-gate policy. |
 | A-463 | **B105 measures Protocol stub bodies and keeps four `TYPE_CHECKING` import blocks plus two narrow selector-cleanup race branches as exact, named coverage exclusions.** The self-qualification and preflight lanes set `allow_excluded = true`, but the test-session hook requires raw `excluded_lines` to equal `tests/fixtures/b105-coverage-exclusions.json` exactly; Coverage.py's default exclusion patterns are replaced with only `pragma: no cover`, and tests invoke each Protocol stub. The 100% whole-target line-and-branch floor remains unchanged for every executable path. | Coverage.py silently excludes Protocol bodies and `TYPE_CHECKING` blocks by default, which made an apparent 100% floor leave source paths outside measurement. Protocol stubs are runtime-invocable and are now exercised. The four remaining imports exist only for annotations and runtime importing would create cycles; the two selector branches handle descriptors the OS or selector has already removed during cleanup. Their exact file and line inventory has an explicit reason and is checked in both directions against raw gate evidence, so a new or moved exclusion cannot silently lower the measured scope. |
+
+## Decided — B109 cold mutation early-stop, 2026-09-27
+
+| id | Decision | Reason |
+|---|---|---|
+| A-464 | **An explicitly opted-in cold native mutation run may classify a candidate as `killed` after a verified, bounded witness proves a collected test's call phase failed and both pytest's session status and Assay's child-process exit status are 1. The candidate's remaining tests may be omitted; survivors and every uncertain case still run the full suite. Full-suite execution remains the default.** | A call-phase failure is existential evidence that this mutant breaks at least one test, so omitted later tests cannot undo that kill. They could otherwise hang or crash; under the opt-in policy that later behavior is not observed and does not replace the witnessed `killed` result. This changes observable R2 outcomes for candidates that would also hang or crash later, so the mode must be distinct in lane/verdict/resume evidence and this limitation must be stated plainly in README, DESIGN-GUIDE, and CONSUMERS.md before adoption. |
